1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,304
On the deep seafloor, an animal walks on
hollow legs full of water.

2
00:00:04,304 --> 00:00:09,825
Yes: sea pigs are real deep-sea animals,
sea cucumbers in the genus Scotoplanes,

3
00:00:09,825 --> 00:00:13,621
and the name is a nickname, not a
relationship to pigs.

4
00:00:13,621 --> 00:00:18,857
Scientists at MBARI, the Monterey Bay
Aquarium Research Institute, have filmed

5
00:00:18,857 --> 00:00:21,744
them alive with remotely operated
vehicles.

6
00:00:21,744 --> 00:00:26,891
Sea pigs have been recorded at many
deep-sea depths, including the abyssal

7
00:00:26,891 --> 00:00:31,579
NOAA's layers of the ocean place the start
of the abyssal zone at 4,000 meters.

8
00:00:31,579 --> 00:00:34,661
That figure tells you roughly where the
habitat is.

9
00:00:34,661 --> 00:00:39,314
It does not tell you how many sea pigs
exist, how long they live, or why they

10
00:00:39,314 --> 00:00:43,363
gather in groups. The animal is confirmed.
Much of its life is not.

11
00:00:43,363 --> 00:00:47,016
Start with the name, because it causes
most of the confusion.

12
00:00:47,016 --> 00:00:51,987
"Sea pig" is a common name, the kind
people give an animal because of how it

13
00:00:51,987 --> 00:00:54,134
It is not a scientific claim.

14
00:00:54,134 --> 00:00:59,541
The scientific identity is this: a sea pig
is a sea cucumber in the genus

15
00:00:59,541 --> 00:01:00,429
Scotoplanes.

16
00:01:00,429 --> 00:01:05,858
Sea cucumbers are echinoderms, the same
group of animals as sea stars, brittle

17
00:01:05,858 --> 00:01:07,389
stars and sea urchins.

18
00:01:07,389 --> 00:01:12,963
They have no relationship to pigs, or to
any mammal. The resemblance is all

19
00:01:12,963 --> 00:01:18,161
A sea pig is plump, pinkish and often
translucent, with a row of stubby legs

20
00:01:18,161 --> 00:01:23,837
underneath and a few soft projections on
its back that can look like ears or horns.

21
00:01:23,837 --> 00:01:27,715
On a dim screen, that shape reads as a
small pink pig.

22
00:01:27,715 --> 00:01:32,704
That is the whole story behind the name,
and it is a story about appearance, not

23
00:01:32,704 --> 00:01:33,203
kinship.

24
00:01:33,203 --> 00:01:37,997
A sea pig is not a pig, in the same way a
sea star is not a star.

25
00:01:37,997 --> 00:01:41,779
So the real question is not whether
something pig-like lives in the deep

26
00:01:41,779 --> 00:01:46,669
It is whether this particular sea cucumber
exists the way the pictures show it.

27
00:01:46,669 --> 00:01:52,328
And that turns into a question about
evidence. Who saw it? With what

28
00:01:52,328 --> 00:01:56,525
Has anyone actually held one, measured it
and put it in a jar?

29
00:01:56,525 --> 00:02:00,021
Part of the doubt comes from a reasonable
instinct.

30
00:02:00,021 --> 00:02:03,859
Sea pigs look like a cartoon, and cartoons
are invented.

31
00:02:03,859 --> 00:02:07,960
But the deep sea is full of bodies that
look odd to a land animal like you,

32
00:02:07,960 --> 00:02:11,843
because the conditions shaping them are
unlike anything at the surface.

33
00:02:11,843 --> 00:02:15,961
There is no sunlight. The water is cold.
Food is scarce.

34
00:02:15,961 --> 00:02:20,595
And the weight of the water column above
presses on everything.

35
00:02:20,595 --> 00:02:25,685
A soft, water-filled body with no hard
skeleton to protect is not a disadvantage

36
00:02:25,685 --> 00:02:27,021
in a place like that.

37
00:02:27,021 --> 00:02:30,134
Strange means unfamiliar. It does not mean
fake.

38
00:02:30,134 --> 00:02:35,192
The way to tell the two apart is not to
judge the picture by how believable it

39
00:02:35,192 --> 00:02:35,581
feels.

40
00:02:35,581 --> 00:02:40,144
It is to ask where the picture came from,
and whether anyone else, using different

41
00:02:40,144 --> 00:02:43,149
equipment on a different day, has seen the
same thing.

42
00:02:43,149 --> 00:02:48,747
Most people who doubt sea pigs have seen
only a still image, often a heavily shared

43
00:02:48,747 --> 00:02:50,771
one, and sometimes a soft toy.

44
00:02:50,771 --> 00:02:53,751
The stronger evidence is moving footage.

45
00:02:53,751 --> 00:02:59,711
Institutions such as MBARI send remotely
operated vehicles, robots tethered to a

46
00:02:59,711 --> 00:03:02,915
ship by a cable, down to the deep
seafloor.

47
00:03:02,915 --> 00:03:07,525
Each remotely operated vehicle carries
lights and a camera, and pilots on the

48
00:03:07,525 --> 00:03:10,579
ship steer it while scientists watch the
live feed.

49
00:03:10,579 --> 00:03:14,897
When a sea pig drifts into frame, the
encounter is recorded and logged with the

50
00:03:14,897 --> 00:03:18,232
depth and location reported by the
vehicle's own instruments.

51
00:03:18,232 --> 00:03:22,865
That footage goes into a video archive
that researchers return to for years,

52
00:03:22,865 --> 00:03:25,181
annotating what appears in each frame.

53
00:03:25,181 --> 00:03:30,403
This matters because a single photograph
can be staged, mislabeled or

54
00:03:30,403 --> 00:03:33,610
A video archive is much harder to fake in
that way.

55
00:03:33,610 --> 00:03:38,389
It holds many encounters, from different
dives, with the same kind of animal

56
00:03:38,389 --> 00:03:43,357
behaving the same way: walking slowly
across soft sediment, its body flexing as

57
00:03:43,357 --> 00:03:43,923
it moves.

58
00:03:43,923 --> 00:03:48,593
When the same thing keeps turning up, the
simplest explanation is that the thing is

59
00:03:48,593 --> 00:03:48,931
there.

60
00:03:48,931 --> 00:03:52,293
MBARI has filmed sea pigs alive in the
deep sea.

61
00:03:52,293 --> 00:03:58,038
Exploration programs such as NOAA Ocean
Exploration film deep-sea sea cucumbers of

62
00:03:58,038 --> 00:04:02,101
many kinds on their dives and share that
footage publicly.

63
00:04:02,101 --> 00:04:06,024
No single clip carries the claim. The
accumulation does.

64
00:04:06,024 --> 00:04:08,144
Footage shows that an animal exists.

65
00:04:08,144 --> 00:04:11,736
It does not, on its own, tell you exactly
what the animal is.

66
00:04:11,736 --> 00:04:13,966
For that, scientists need specimens.

67
00:04:13,966 --> 00:04:18,797
A remotely operated vehicle can collect a
specimen with a suction sampler or a

68
00:04:18,797 --> 00:04:23,690
robotic arm, and older expeditions dredged
animals up from the bottom with nets

69
00:04:23,690 --> 00:04:25,363
dragged along the seafloor.

70
00:04:25,363 --> 00:04:29,256
Once the animal is on deck, it is
photographed, and then the team must

71
00:04:29,256 --> 00:04:33,093
preserve it so it can be studied in a
laboratory and kept in a museum

72
00:04:33,093 --> 00:04:36,931
collection, where it can be checked again
long after the voyage ends.

73
00:04:36,931 --> 00:04:41,904
Sea cucumber identification leans on
details that no camera captures well.

74
00:04:41,904 --> 00:04:47,147
Many sea cucumbers have ossicles, tiny
pieces of mineral embedded in the skin,

75
00:04:47,147 --> 00:04:49,768
and their shapes differ between groups.

76
00:04:49,768 --> 00:04:54,577
Taxonomists examine the ossicles under a
microscope, describe the tube feet and

77
00:04:54,577 --> 00:04:59,021
other structures, and compare against
described species, each of which is

78
00:04:59,021 --> 00:05:02,552
anchored to a physical type specimen held
in a collection.

79
00:05:02,552 --> 00:05:05,408
Increasingly, they sequence DNA as well.

80
00:05:05,408 --> 00:05:10,048
In other words, a name tied to a physical
type, not to a picture.

81
00:05:10,048 --> 00:05:15,536
That is why the genus Scotoplanes is a
defined thing rather than a loose label.

82
00:05:15,536 --> 00:05:19,953
There are preserved animals in collections
that anyone with the right training can

83
00:05:19,953 --> 00:05:21,408
re-examine and argue about.

84
00:05:21,408 --> 00:05:26,459
What was actually measured? By whom? Over
what period? How big was the sample?

85
00:05:26,459 --> 00:05:30,951
Once you accept that sea pigs exist, the
next fair question is how they walk,

86
00:05:30,951 --> 00:05:35,211
because the legs are both the most
pig-like part and the least intuitive.

87
00:05:35,211 --> 00:05:38,373
They are not legs in the sense that your
legs are legs.

88
00:05:38,373 --> 00:05:41,122
They are not jointed legs with bones
inside.

89
00:05:41,122 --> 00:05:46,370
They are water-filled tube feet, part of a
system that echinoderms share, called the

90
00:05:46,370 --> 00:05:47,744
water vascular system.

91
00:05:47,744 --> 00:05:50,744
It is a network of internal canals filled
with fluid.

92
00:05:50,744 --> 00:05:55,273
By shifting that fluid and contracting
muscles, the animal can extend or shorten

93
00:05:55,273 --> 00:06:00,028
each tube foot, a little like pressing on
one end of a water balloon to push out the

94
00:06:00,028 --> 00:06:00,368
other.

95
00:06:00,368 --> 00:06:05,537
In most sea cucumbers, the tube feet are
small and numerous, used for gripping

96
00:06:05,537 --> 00:06:06,133
surfaces.

97
00:06:06,133 --> 00:06:10,724
In sea pigs, some of them are enlarged
into stubby walking feet along the

98
00:06:10,724 --> 00:06:15,440
underside, and the soft projections on the
back are modified tube feet too.

99
00:06:15,440 --> 00:06:19,179
How is it known that this is hydraulic,
and not simply muscle?

100
00:06:19,179 --> 00:06:24,571
Because the anatomy of dissected specimens
shows the canals connecting to the tube

101
00:06:24,571 --> 00:06:29,307
feet, the same plan found across
echinoderms, from sea stars to urchins.

102
00:06:29,307 --> 00:06:33,995
The walking is visible in footage. The
mechanism is visible on the dissecting

103
00:06:33,995 --> 00:06:39,385
Two different kinds of evidence, gathered
in two different places, describe the same

104
00:06:39,385 --> 00:06:39,835
animal.

105
00:06:39,835 --> 00:06:44,053
To understand what sea pigs eat, you need
a sense of where they live.

106
00:06:44,053 --> 00:06:48,910
They have been recorded across a wide band
of deep-sea depths, from continental

107
00:06:48,910 --> 00:06:53,827
slopes down to abyssal plains, and the
published ranges vary between species and

108
00:06:53,827 --> 00:06:54,811
between surveys.

109
00:06:54,811 --> 00:07:00,139
NOAA's layers of the ocean place the
abyssal zone beginning at 4,000 meters.

110
00:07:00,139 --> 00:07:04,144
Sunlight does not reach anywhere near that
far. No plants grow there.

111
00:07:04,144 --> 00:07:09,272
Almost all food arrives from above, as a
slow fall of dead plankton, waste and

112
00:07:09,272 --> 00:07:13,019
other organic particles that scientists
call marine snow.

113
00:07:13,019 --> 00:07:16,437
It settles onto the seafloor as a thin,
uneven film.

114
00:07:16,437 --> 00:07:18,156
Sea pigs are deposit feeders.

115
00:07:18,156 --> 00:07:22,899
They use tentacles around the mouth to
gather the surface layer of sediment, and

116
00:07:22,899 --> 00:07:25,744
their gut extracts the organic material
from it.

117
00:07:25,744 --> 00:07:30,349
Studies of deep-sea sea cucumbers suggest
that many of them favor the freshest

118
00:07:30,349 --> 00:07:34,187
material, the part of marine snow that has
only recently settled.

119
00:07:34,187 --> 00:07:37,174
This is one reason ecologists pay
attention to them.

120
00:07:37,174 --> 00:07:41,826
Animals like sea pigs work through the
seafloor's surface, and they are among the

121
00:07:41,826 --> 00:07:45,445
animals that use food arriving from the
sunlit ocean far above.

122
00:07:45,445 --> 00:07:50,411
Seafloor surveys have found that sea
cucumbers can make up a large share of the

123
00:07:50,411 --> 00:07:53,365
visible animal life on parts of the deep
floor.

124
00:07:53,365 --> 00:07:58,704
Exactly how large varies from place to
place, and from one survey method to

125
00:07:58,704 --> 00:08:04,079
One of the striking things in seafloor
footage is that sea pigs are often seen in

126
00:08:04,079 --> 00:08:06,667
groups, sometimes many in view at once.

127
00:08:06,667 --> 00:08:10,971
In some recordings, many of them appear to
be facing the same direction.

128
00:08:10,971 --> 00:08:15,296
Researchers have suggested that facing
into the current might help them detect

129
00:08:15,296 --> 00:08:18,901
food arriving from upstream, the way a
scent carries on a breeze.

130
00:08:18,901 --> 00:08:23,686
That is a reasonable hypothesis. It is not
a demonstrated fact.

131
00:08:23,686 --> 00:08:25,584
Footage records position.

132
00:08:25,584 --> 00:08:30,774
It does not record purpose, and a group in
one frame may have formed around a patch

133
00:08:30,774 --> 00:08:35,715
of food, in response to the current, or
for a reason no one has yet considered.

134
00:08:35,715 --> 00:08:39,508
So the checklist is uneven. Seen in
groups: yes.

135
00:08:39,508 --> 00:08:44,725
Facing the same direction: in some
footage. Why they gather: open.

136
00:08:44,725 --> 00:08:46,621
How many exist: unknown.

137
00:08:46,621 --> 00:08:50,552
Something similar applies to the small
animals that have been seen with them.

138
00:08:50,552 --> 00:08:54,632
Footage has shown small crabs sheltering
under or beside sea pigs.

139
00:08:54,632 --> 00:08:58,280
That association is real in the sense that
it was recorded.

140
00:08:58,280 --> 00:09:03,103
Whether it helps the crab, harms the sea
pig, or makes no difference to either is a

141
00:09:03,103 --> 00:09:06,648
separate question, and one that a camera
alone cannot answer.

142
00:09:06,648 --> 00:09:11,016
As you watch, notice which images are
footage and which are stills.

143
00:09:11,016 --> 00:09:15,573
Notice which shots come from remotely
operated vehicles, with the lights casting

144
00:09:15,573 --> 00:09:20,131
a cone onto soft sediment, and which are
illustrations made to show you a shape.

145
00:09:20,131 --> 00:09:25,119
The claim that sea pigs are real rests on
footage and specimens, not a single

146
00:09:25,119 --> 00:09:25,832
photograph.

147
00:09:25,832 --> 00:09:30,916
When a drawing appears, it is there to
show the outline people recognize, not to

148
00:09:30,916 --> 00:09:32,696
stand in as evidence for it.

149
00:09:32,696 --> 00:09:38,257
This evidence establishes that sea pigs
exist, what group they belong to, how they

150
00:09:38,257 --> 00:09:40,360
move and broadly what they eat.

151
00:09:40,360 --> 00:09:42,793
It does not establish how many there are.

152
00:09:42,793 --> 00:09:47,482
The deep seafloor is vast and only a small
fraction of it has ever been seen by

153
00:09:47,482 --> 00:09:52,407
cameras, so any population estimate would
be an extrapolation from a limited number

154
00:09:52,407 --> 00:09:52,941
of sites.

155
00:09:52,941 --> 00:09:55,936
There is no reliable figure for how long
they live.

156
00:09:55,936 --> 00:10:00,457
How many species belong in the genus
Scotoplanes, and how they are related to

157
00:10:00,457 --> 00:10:05,095
each other, depends on how specimens are
classified and on genetic work that is

158
00:10:05,095 --> 00:10:05,917
still limited.

159
00:10:05,917 --> 00:10:09,031
And the reasons sea pigs gather remain a
hypothesis.

160
00:10:09,031 --> 00:10:13,403
When you meet confident numbers about sea
pigs, ask where they came from.

161
00:10:13,403 --> 00:10:14,796
So yes, they are real.

162
00:10:14,796 --> 00:10:19,482
Not a pig, and not a hoax, but a sea
cucumber walking on water-filled feet

163
00:10:19,482 --> 00:10:24,865
across the deep seafloor, filmed alive and
kept in collections, with most of its life

164
00:10:24,865 --> 00:10:26,005
still out of view.
